<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Guelph Hydrants &mdash; Reference Layer for OSM Editors</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="index.css">
<link rel="stylesheet"
      href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"
      integrity="sha256-p4NxAoJBhIIN+hmNHrzRCf9tD/miZyoHS5obTRR9BMY="
      crossorigin="">
<script defer src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"
        integrity="sha256-20nQCchB9co0qIjJZRGuk2/Z9VM+kNiyxNV1lvTlZBo="
        crossorigin=""></script>
<script defer src="index.js"></script>
<script data-goatcounter="https://osm-guelph.goatcounter.com/count" async src="//gc.zgo.at/count.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
<main>

<h1>Guelph Hydrants</h1>
<p class="lead">
  A reference layer of the fire hydrants published by the City of Guelph
  &mdash; <strong>{{HYDRANT_COUNT}}</strong> municipal hydrants &mdash; ready to
  add to the <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org">OpenStreetMap</a> editors
  <strong>iD</strong> and <strong>JOSM</strong> while you map.
</p>
<p class="meta">City data: {{DATA_DATE}} &middot; tiles rebuilt: {{BUILD_DATE}}</p>

<div class="attribution">
  <a href="{{LICENSE_URL}}">{{LICENSE_NOTICE}}</a>
  Source: <a href="{{DATASET_PAGE}}">Hydrants</a>.
</div>

<h2>Preview</h2>
<div id="preview-map" data-raster-url="{{RASTER_URL}}"></div>
<p class="meta">
  The raster layer drawn over OpenStreetMap: a red dot per City hydrant, from
  zoom 15.
</p>

<div class="attribution">
  <strong>Coverage vs OpenStreetMap:</strong> see which City hydrants are
  <a href="gaps/">missing from OSM, misplaced, mapped twice, or missing their
  type, colour or position</a> &mdash; a mapper's worklist with an interactive
  map and table.
</div>

<h2>Two layers &mdash; which to use</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Vector layer</th>
    <td>Interactive. Click a hydrant to read the City's fields
        (<code>hydrant_id</code>, <code>location_id</code>, <code>model</code>,
        <code>objectid</code>). Best in <strong>iD</strong>.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <th>Raster layer</th>
    <td>Plain dots, no labels. A readable backdrop. Best in
        <strong>JOSM</strong>.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<div class="note">
  <strong>Do not copy the City's numbers into <code>ref</code>.</strong>
  <code>hydrant_id</code> and <code>location_id</code> are the City's asset
  numbers; nothing on the hydrant carries them, so no mapper can check one. The
  dot tells you where to look. What goes into OSM is what you can see:
  <code>emergency=fire_hydrant</code>, <code>fire_hydrant:type</code>,
  <code>colour</code> and <code>fire_hydrant:position</code>.
</div>

<h2>Add to iD</h2>
<h3>Vector layer (interactive)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open the iD editor and press <kbd>U</kbd> for the <em>Map Data</em> panel.</li>
  <li>Under <em>Custom Map Data</em>, open the settings and paste this URL:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">{{VECTOR_URL}}</pre>
<p>Zoom in, then click any hydrant to see its fields in the inspector.</p>

<h3>Raster layer</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open the <em>Background</em> panel and choose <em>Custom</em>.</li>
  <li>Paste this URL template:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">{{RASTER_URL}}</pre>

<h2>Add to JOSM</h2>
<h3>Raster layer (recommended for JOSM)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open <em>Preferences &rarr; Imagery</em> and click <strong>+TMS</strong>.</li>
  <li>Paste this entry (the <code>tms[15,17]</code> prefix sets the zoom range;
      JOSM scales it up past z17):</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">tms[15,17]:{{RASTER_URL_JOSM}}</pre>
<p>
  Give it a name such as <em>Guelph Hydrants</em>, click OK, then enable
  it from the <em>Imagery</em> menu. Note JOSM's placeholder is
  <code>{zoom}</code>, not <code>{z}</code>.
</p>

<h3>Vector layer (optional)</h3>
<p>
  JOSM can also add the vector tiles via <em>Preferences &rarr; Imagery</em> &rarr;
  <strong>+MVT</strong>:
</p>
<pre class="url">{{VECTOR_URL_JOSM}}</pre>

<h2>Notes</h2>
<ul>
  <li>The City's layer is its own hydrants. Hydrants on private land (a campus,
      a plaza, an industrial yard) are not in it, so an OSM hydrant with no dot
      under it is not wrong &mdash; the gap page lists those as &ldquo;OSM
      only&rdquo; for a look, not a fix.</li>
  <li>The City's points are accurate: OSM hydrants placed from survey sit a
      median 1.4&nbsp;m from them. A hydrant is verified by survey or
      street-level imagery, not from aerial imagery.</li>
  <li>Vector tiles are pre-rendered for z12&ndash;19; raster tiles for
      z15&ndash;17 (the editor overzooms them further).</li>
  <li>Tiles are refreshed weekly from the City's service.</li>
  <li>This is a <strong>reference</strong> layer, not an import &mdash; add a
      hydrant to OpenStreetMap when you have seen it.</li>
</ul>

<footer>
  <a href="https://github.com/{{GITHUB_REPO}}">Project source on GitHub</a>
